Presidential elections were held in [[Montenegro]] on 15 April 2018.<ref>[https://www.nytimes.com/aponline/2018/01/19/world/europe/ap-eu-montenegro-presidential-election.html Montenegro Presidential Election Set for April 15] New York Times, 19 January 2018</ref> Former [[Prime Minister of Montenegro|Prime Minister]] [[Milo Đukanović]], leader of the ruling [[Democratic Party of Socialists|DPS]] was elected as new President of Montenegro in the first round.

==Electoral system==
The [[President of Montenegro|president]] is elected using the [[two-round system]]; if no candidate receives a majority of the vote in the first round, a run-off is held two weeks later.<ref>[http://www.nsd.uib.no/european_election_database/country/montenegro/presidential_elections.html Montenegro: Presidential Elections] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171020033607/http://www.nsd.uib.no/european_election_database/country/montenegro/presidential_elections.html |date=20 October 2017 }} European Elections Database</ref> In order to submit their candidacy to the State Electoral Commission, potential candidates needed to collect 7,993 signatures.<ref>[https://www.rtcg.me/vijesti/politika/186801/milickovic-ozvanicio-kandidaturu.html Miličković ozvaničio kandidaturu] RTCG, 6 December 2017</ref> President is elected every five years, and is eligible for two terms.

==Campaign==
The incumbent [[President of Montenegro|president]], [[Filip Vujanović]], was ineligible for re-election, having already served three terms as president.

President of the ruling [[Democratic Party of Socialists of Montenegro|DPS]], [[Milo Đukanović]], who was touted by the media as the election favourite, initially rejected the possibility of running for president.<ref>[http://www.vijesti.me/vijesti/dukanovic-ne-zeli-na-predsjednicke-izbore-dps-ima-vise-dobrih-kandidata-959419 Đukanović ne želi na predsjedničke izbore: DPS ima više dobrih kandidata] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180426100330/http://www.vijesti.me/vijesti/dukanovic-ne-zeli-na-predsjednicke-izbore-dps-ima-vise-dobrih-kandidata-959419 |date=26 April 2018 }} Vijesti, 20 October 2017</ref>

In March 2018, Đukanović confirmed that he would run for president,<ref>[https://www.rts.rs/page/stories/sr/story/11/region/3073971/rtcg-milo-djukanovic-kandidat-na-predsednickim-izborima.html Milo Đukanović kandidat na predsedničkim izborima], RTS</ref> supported by DPS' coalition partner, the [[Liberal Party of Montenegro|Liberal Party]], as well as other subjects in the government; [[Social Democrats of Montenegro|Social Democrats]], [[Croatian Civic Initiative]], the [[New Democratic Power - FORCA|New Democratic Power]], [[Democratic Union of Albanians]] and the [[Bosniak Party]].

After a series of unsuccessful attempts by the entire opposition to nominate a common candidate, it was clear that the opposition would have more candidates. On 9 March 2018, the presidency of right-wing [[Democratic Front (Montenegro)|Democratic Front]] alliance decides to support the candidacy of independent candidate [[Mladen Bojanić]], which was previously proposed by the centrist [[Democratic Montenegro]] and the centre-left [[United Reform Action]]. Presidency of the newly-formed centre-right [[United Montenegro]] has decided to support the previously announced candidacy of the party leader [[Goran Danilović]], later Presidency of centre-left [[Social Democratic Party of Montenegro|Social Democratic Party]] has consistently endorsed candidacy of [[Parliament of Montenegro|MP]] [[Draginja Vuksanović]], making her first ever female presidential candidate, with support of centre-right [[DEMOS (Montenegro)|DEMOS]] party led by [[Miodrag Lekić]], common opposition presidential candidate at the [[2013 Montenegrin presidential election|previous election]].

From the rest of the opposition have unsuccessfully called upon the SDP and Danilović's United Montenegro party to withdraw their candidacies and support independent Bojanić as an opposition common candidate, which they have refused. From SDP replied that Bojanić was not a completely independent candidate, alluding to his alleged support for the Democratic Front list for the [[2016 Montenegrin parliamentary election|2016 parliamentary election]]. On 21 March Danilović ultimately withdrew his candidacy and supported Bojanić's candidature.

==Candidates==
Montenegrin State Electoral Commission (DIK) confirmed seven candidates. Candidate numbers were decided using a random draw on 28 March.<ref>[https://rtcg.me/vijesti/predsjednicki-izbori-2018/198394/ovo-je-redosljed-kandidata-na-listicu.html Ovo je redosljed kandidata na listiću], RTCG</ref>

